{"type":"agent_end","messages":[{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are an expert coding assistant operating inside pi, a coding agent harness. You help users by reading files, executing commands, editing code, and writing new files.","tools":"<tools>\n(none)\n\nIn addition to the tools above, you may have access to other custom tools depending on the project.\n</tools>","rules":"<rules>\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>","docs":"<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about pi itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: C:\\Users\\codexlab\\.pi\\agent\\install\\releases\\0.87.1\\node_modules\\@earendil-works\\pi-coding-agent\\README.md\n- Additional docs: C:\\Users\\codexlab\\.pi\\agent\\install\\releases\\0.87.1\\node_modules\\@earendil-works\\pi-coding-agent\\docs\n- Examples: C:\\Users\\codexlab\\.pi\\agent\\install\\releases\\0.87.1\\node_modules\\@earendil-works\\pi-coding-agent\\examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), pi packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md)\n- When working on pi topics, read the docs and examples, and follow .md cross-references before implementing\n- Always read pi .md files completely and follow links to related docs (e.g., tui.md for TUI API details)\n</docs>","project_context":"<project_context>\nProject-specific instructions and guidelines:\n\n<project_instructions path=\"C:\\Users\\codexlab\\.pi\\agent\\AGENTS.md\">\n# Global working rules\n\n## Package manager: pnpm is the default\n\nUse **pnpm** for all JavaScript/TypeScript dependency work on this machine.\n\nDo not run `npm install`, `npm i`, `npm ci`, `npx`, or `yarn`. Do not create or\ncommit `package-lock.json` or `yarn.lock`. The only lockfile that should exist is\n`pnpm-lock.yaml`.\n\nThe only exception is a repository that already contains a `package-lock.json`\nor `yarn.lock` and explicitly pins that tool. In that case, ask before switching\nit, or convert it with `pnpm import` (from `package-lock.json`).\n\n### Command mapping\n\n| Instead of | Use |\n| --- | --- |\n| `npm install` | `pnpm install` |\n| `npm install <pkg>` | `pnpm add <pkg>` |\n| `npm install -D <pkg>` | `pnpm add -D <pkg>` |\n| `npm uninstall <pkg>` | `pnpm remove <pkg>` |\n| `npm ci` | `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` |\n| `npx <tool>` | `pnpm dlx <tool>` |\n| `npm run <script>` | `pnpm <script>` (or `pnpm run <script>`) |\n| `npm outdated` | `pnpm outdated` |\n| `npm ls` | `pnpm ls` |\n| `npm audit` | `pnpm audit` |\n\nPrefer `pnpm add <pkg> -w` for adding a dependency to the workspace root of a\nmonorepo, and `pnpm --filter <pkg> <script>` to run a script in one workspace\npackage.\n\n### Why\n\npnpm keeps a single content-addressable store on D: and populates each project\nwith hardlinks, instead of making npm copy a full private copy of every\ndependency into every project. This keeps C: (small, 40 GB free) from filling\nup. Caching and the store are already configured to live on D:.\n\n### Pin the package manager version\n\nWhen creating a new `package.json`, include:\n\n```json\n\"packageManager\": \"pnpm@11.25.0\"\n```\n\npnpm reads this and self-manages that exact version, so the version is\nreproducible for the user and for CI without needing a global install.\n(`corepack enable` is NOT available on this machine, it needs Administrator\nrights. pnpm's own `packageManager` support covers this.)\n\n### esbuild: allow its build script\n\nVite depends on esbuild, which ships a native binary via a postinstall script.\npnpm blocks dependency build scripts by default and fails with\n`[ERR_PNPM_IGNORED_BUILDS] Ignored build scripts: esbuild@<version>`.\n\nAllow it once per project by committing a `pnpm-workspace.yaml` next to\n`package.json`:\n\n```yaml\nallowBuilds:\n  esbuild: true\n```\n\nDo not ignore this error. It is easy to misread as cosmetic, because esbuild\ncan still `import` fine and `esbuild.transform()` can appear to work, while a\nlater real build fails or uses a stale binary. Check for it whenever a pnpm\ncommand exits non-zero.\n\n### Converting an existing npm project\n\nFor a repo that already has `package-lock.json` and npm-style `node_modules`:\n\n1. Add `\"packageManager\": \"pnpm@<version>\"` to `package.json`.\n2. `pnpm install` to regenerate `node_modules` in pnpm layout. This\n   restructures `node_modules` in place, so do not run it while a dev server or\n   other process has that directory open. Note that pnpm may run this\n   implicitly during `pnpm exec` / status checks, so adding the\n   `packageManager` field can trigger it unexpectedly.\n3. Verify with a real `pnpm build`, not just `pnpm dev`. A dev server can keep\n   serving from an in-memory module graph even after `node_modules` changes\n   underneath it, which makes it a false signal.\n4. Delete `package-lock.json` once the build passes, so only `pnpm-lock.yaml`\n   remains.\n\nTo re-use an existing lockfile instead of re-resolving, use `pnpm import`.\n\n### Central store locations\n\n| What | Where |\n| --- | --- |\n| pnpm content store | `D:\\pnpm-store` |\n| pnpm metadata cache | `D:\\pnpm-cache` |\n| npm cache | `D:\\npm-cache` |\n\nIf a project is on a different volume than `D:\\pnpm-store`, pnpm cannot hardlink\nand silently falls back to copying. Keep projects on D: where possible, or accept\nthe copy for the occasional C: project.\n\n## Vite: keep the dep-optimizer cache off the project drive\n\nVite defaults `cacheDir` to `node_modules/.vite`, so its pre-bundle cache grows\ninside the repo. Point it at `D:` instead, keyed per project:\n\n```ts\n// Keep the dep-optimizer cache on D: and out of the repo, keyed per project.\nconst viteCache = \"D:/Coding/.cache/vite/my-project\";\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  cacheDir: viteCache,\n});\n```\n\nDo NOT use `os.tmpdir()` for this. On this machine `%TEMP%` resolves to\n`C:\\Users\\codexlab\\AppData\\Local\\Temp`, which is exactly the drive the user wants\nkept clean. Use a literal `D:/` path, or build it from a shared constant.\n\nKeep a unique per-project name in that path. A single shared directory will make\nconcurrent builds of different projects overwrite each other's optimized deps.\n\nSame idea applies to other tools that default to a repo-local cache: point them\nat `D:\\` or `%TEMP%` if they offer a cache directory option.\n\n## Blender: driving the live scene over MCP\n\nThe user works in Blender 5.2.2 LTS, installed at `D:\\Blender\\blender.exe`\nand **not on PATH**. The `blender` MCP server (`mcp-for-blender`, via `uvx`)\nis already wired up and exposes 36 tools, including PolyHaven / Sketchfab /\nPoly Pizza asset search and Hyper3D Rodin, Hunyuan3D and Tripo generation. Do\nnot propose replacing it; it is a superset of the stock Blender Lab server.\n\n### Start of every Blender task\n\n1. `blender_get_addon_status` — reports `blender_version` and whether the\n   bridge is actually reachable. If it says \"Could not connect to Blender\",\n   the addon is stopped or Blender is closed; say so instead of guessing.\n2. `blender_get_scene_info` — see what already exists before touching anything.\n   Never assume a default startup scene.\n\nKnown issue: the installed addon reports **protocol 9 while the server expects\n11**. Fix with `uvx mcp-for-blender install-addon`, then restart Blender.\n\n### Mechanics that will bite you\n\n- **`execute_blender_code` only returns `print()` output.** A trailing\n  expression is swallowed and you get `Code executed successfully:` with\n  nothing. Always end with an explicit `print()` of your report.\n- **Re-declare your helpers in every call.** Do not assume a previous call's\n  namespace survived.\n- **One stage per call, and keep each call small.** A crash mid-script leaves\n  the scene in a *partially applied* state. After any error, inspect with\n  `get_scene_info` before retrying — never blind-retry, and never assume how\n  far the previous call got.\n- **Filter by `o.type == 'MESH'` before iterating objects by name prefix.**\n  Empties and helpers are named similarly to the meshes they group. A loop over\n  `startswith(\"Typebar_\")` also caught the `Typebar_Basket` empty and crashed on\n  a string parse. Prefer `name.rsplit(\"_c\", 1)[1]` over `split(\"_c\")[1]`.\n- **Parenting:** author child coordinates in the parent's local space and leave\n  `matrix_parent_inverse` as identity. Setting it to the parent's inverse pins\n  the child in world space instead of making it ride the parent.\n\n### Verify before reporting done\n\nAssert numerically *and* look at a render. These are not interchangeable — a\nreal build passed all 9 numeric checks while the render showed a detached foot\nfloating on the floor, a keyboard hanging off the front of the body, and a\npaper table wearing the paper material.\n\n- Numeric pass: counts, dimensions, real-world scale, `min_z == 0` on the floor,\n  parenting behaves, every mesh has a material, nothing detached.\n- Visual pass: `bpy.ops.render.render(write_still=True)` to a temp path, then\n  **read the image back** and inspect it.\n- If the visual pass fails, fix and re-render. Do not report success on the\n  numeric pass alone.\n\n### Files and scenes\n\n- 3D files belong in the project they belong to. **Do not reorganise, move, or\n  invent folders by type** (no `3d/` folder, no central asset store).\n- Do not save a `.blend` unless asked. If a task is long enough that losing it\n  would hurt, ask to save early, or offer a save point after each stage.\n\n\n## Tests\n\nWe build for success. We expect failure. We build a robust system to facilitate\nquick restarts and investigate the rubble, so we can fix it (internal), or catch\nit (external).\n\n**Allowed tests:**\n\n- **Unit tests (Builder)** — allowed to test its own work while writing it, but\n  we do not leave them running or deliver them as part of the slice.\n- **Functional tests (Tester)** — allowed to test milestone functionality, and\n  defined **after a milestone completes only**. These are the only tests that may\n  run after future builds, because they guarantee the stability of the delivered\n  product.\n\nWe never create other tests. Instead, we repair bugs or respond to outside factors.\n\n**Never write code to test code.** It can itself be buggy, and the recursion proves nothing.\n\n**We test code only to keep accepted functionality stable.** Those are the\nTester's tests, written to establish whether a milestone is reached. They are\nthe tests we keep.\n\n**We are the production environment, not the arbiter.** What a team of actors\nbuilds with the tools is not ours to judge. Never built, about any product:\n\n- **test** — code that tests code\n- **check** — a pass between two build steps\n- **validate** — a validation pass between two build steps\n- **recompute and compare** — testing a worker's own declaration against itself mid-build\n- **gate** — a pass/fail gate inside the loop\n- **review** — anyone looking at a slice\n- **score** — ranking a worker, a slice or a model\n- **rank** — ordering anything by preference\n- **evaluate** — deciding whether a product is good\n\n**Our job is that the actors are safe, and that the machinery works as\npredicted.** So we monitor the machinery — that it launches, sweeps, recovers\nand reports what it did. We never monitor what they made.\n\n**Issues inside our system** → fix it. Don't provide tests, just fix the code\nuntil it works.\n\n**Issues outside our system** (provider, model, runtime) → prepare for it, build\ncode to deal with it, since we cannot build code to fix it. We still do not test it.\n\n## Windows notes\n\n- Shell is Git Bash (`/c/...` style paths); Node tools expect `C:\\...` or\n  `D:/...` forward-slash paths. Both work.\n- `mklink /J` (directory junction) can move a directory to another volume\n  without Administrator rights. Useful escape hatch if a tool insists on\n  writing into the repo.\n\n</project_instructions>\n</project_context>","cwd":"<cwd>\nD:/Coding/progressDesk/projects/drop/rounds/r4\n</cwd>"},"timestamp":1790803927431},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<file name=\"D:\\Coding\\progressDesk\\projects\\drop\\rounds\\r4\\context-pack.md\">\n## task\r\n\r\nReply with exactly one line: acknowledged.\n</file>\n"}],"timestamp":1790803927430},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"acknowledged."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"stealth/space-bunny-alpha","usage":{"input":54,"output":4,"cacheRead":3174,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":3232,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1790803927451,"responseId":"gen-1790803927-gtuUXET5WVwL5NTBEbsK","rawStopReason":"stop"}],"willRetry":false}
